Q: Incorrect TypeScript error in function returning never A: <p>It's a little bit surprising as an end-user but it turns out this is expected behaviour.</p>
<p>This is called out at the end of the <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/pull/32695" rel="nofollow noreferrer">pull request that implemented the &quot;Assertions in control flow analysis&quot; behaviour</a>.</p>
<p>Specifically, at the bottom of the original comment it has this statement (bolded emphasis mine):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A function call is analyzed as an assertion call or <strong>never-returning call</strong> when...</p>
<ul>
<li>each identifier in the function name references an entity with an explicit type...</li>
</ul>
<p>An entity is considered to have an explicit type when it is <strong>declared as a function</strong>, method, class or namespace, <strong>or as a variable, parameter or property with an explicit type annotation</strong>. (This particular rule exists so that control flow analysis of potential assertion calls doesn't circularly trigger further analysis.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So the fix is to make sure your <code>croak()</code> function has an explicit type.</p>
<p>Here are some ways of explicitly typing the function so it participates in assertion control flow (note, this is not an exhaustive list):</p>
<pre><code>// ⚠️ this function will not trigger the assertion check because it has an implicit type
const croak = (errMsg: string): never =&gt; {
    console.error('CROAK' + ': ' + errMsg)
    Deno.exit()
}

// ✅ explicit type declaration (inline)
const croak2: (errMsg: string) =&gt; never = (errMsg: string): never =&gt; {
    console.error('CROAK' + ': ' + errMsg)
    Deno.exit()
}

// ✅ explcit type declaration (via type)
type NeverFn = (errMsg: string) =&gt; never;
const croak3: NeverFn = (errMsg: string): never =&gt; {
    console.error('CROAK' + ': ' + errMsg)
    Deno.exit()
}

// ✅ function declaration
function croak4(errMsg: string): never {
    console.error('CROAK' + ': ' + errMsg)
    Deno.exit()
}
</code></pre>
<p>This was also answered previously here, but didn't have a detailed explanation of why it's required or some of the alternatve ways of having an explicitly typed function: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/73661697/81723">https://stackoverflow.com/a/73661697/81723</a></p>
